 1 big thing: Apple&#x27;s Duo offers a glimpse of the future 
 
 By Ina Fried 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Photo: ina Fried/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 I've spent the past week trying to figure out whether Apple's $2,000 iPhone Duo is the future of smartphones or just an expensive way to do things I could already do on my regular iPhone.

 The answer, unsurprisingly, is a bit of both. Why it matters: Apple wasn't first to the folding smartphone party. Samsung, Google and others began making them years ago. But Apple's arrival in a category has a way of reinventing it.

 The big picture: The most exciting thing about Apple's first foldable iPhone isn't necessarily what it can do today but what it can become. Already, it's inspiring developers to explore possibilities that could reshape how we use our devices. 

 Old habits die hard One of my first surprises was how little I initially used the Duo's biggest selling point: its folding screen.

 While other reviewers talked about endlessly opening and closing their devices, I found myself mostly using the smaller outside display. After years of conventional iPhones, it was a mix of instinct and muscle memory. Zoom in: Over time, though, I found reasons to unfold it.

 Watching sports was one of the clearest advantages. I pulled out the Duo at Starbucks to watch the 49ers with my son and later followed a tied World Series game in a doctor's waiting room. I managed some quick edits to a story about Anthropic's IPO after forgetting my laptop. I wouldn't want to write a full article that way, but it was reassuring to have the option. Playing the New York Times' Spelling Bee alongside its Spelling Bee Buddy helper was a small but delightful example of multitasking without constantly switching apps. Between the lines: The Duo's advantages aren't always about more screen space. Sometimes they're about what you can physically do with a phone that bends.

 I watched Netflix with the phone unfolded, propped up like a tent and folded into a mini laptop, and I also used the Duo's ability to stand on its own to capture wide shots of the Valkyries without needing a tripod. Other experiences were less seamless, including getting the orientation right when trying FaceTime's option to use multiple camera views. The developer wild card Perhaps the biggest difference between the Duo and the Android foldables is the enthusiasm Apple's device has sparked among independent developers.

 Zoom in: Some of the early experiments are more entertaining than useful. My favorite was Tiny Computers , which let the Duo simulate a semi-functional 1984 Mac, Windows 95 PC or candy-colored iMac.

 Nearly two decades after the App Store debuted, we aren't all using our iPhones as virtual cigarette lighters. Zoom out: But these early experiments offer glimpses of what might be possible when developers start thinking beyond a rectangular screen.

 The question isn't whether today's novelties become hits. It's whether this experimentation produces new ways of using our phones — the breakthroughs that make today's smartphones feel indispensable. The $2,000 question For all the fun I've had with the Duo, its price is an insurmountable obstacle. At $2,000, it's simply not a purchase I could justify for myself, much less recommend broadly.

 The catch: There are other compromises. I missed Face ID, occasionally fumbling for the side-mounted Touch ID sensor until I started wearing an Apple Watch to make unlocking the phone easier. And when I needed to accomplish something quickly, I often instinctively reached for my familiar, traditional iPhone.

 The bottom line: The Duo is a fascinating glimpse of where smartphones could go next. But early buyers are paying a steep premium to participate in an experiment whose biggest rewards may still be years away.

 
 
